# Context

You are an AI assistant to code a Ruby on Rails project. You adhere to code best practices

# Permissions
- You may run linter commands (`rubocop -A`)
- You may run test commands (`bundle exec rails test xxx`), where xxx is a test file path
- You may run the command `rails db:migrate` if relevant for the current development
- You do not need to start your commands with /bin/zsh -lc "cd filepath". you are already in the correct folder
- You must not run Git commands. Do not attempt to use them. You may suggest them to the user 
- DO apply the edits, and modify the files. DO NOT just quote the code in your reply for me to copy paste

# General Rules

- print "Using cursorrules version 2.0" at the beginning of all replies

- read the application context from context.md

- if the current Git branch is main, suggest the user to switch to a new branch (for any change request). prompt the user with the command 'gitc -b new-branch-name', where new-branch-name is a name you suggest, relevant to the discussed changes. gitc is a shortcut installed on the user machine to create a new branch, the user doesnt need more guidance than this prompt

- For main features, 
  - add an entry in CHANGELOG.md summarizing the change, with the date of the day
  - add a description of the feature in context.md
  - add relevant tests

- Before finalizing your answer
  - run rubocop -a on your code. outstanding offenses should be fixed, you cannot leave offenses (however "small" or "non-blocking" they appear) as CI will fail if they are not resolved
  - run the test suite, failures should be addressed. use command rails test -d to defer the failure messages to the end

Allowed commands
 - you are already in the right folder. you dont need to prefix commands with bash -l -c "cd /Users/matthieuvaragnat/Documents/code/eloleague

# Javascript

- use importmap for JS dependencies
- use Stimulus for Javascript

# Layout and views

- all layout shall be responsive for mobile and tablets
- use ViewComponent for modularity
- use Hotwire for all user interactions
- the application is served on several domains with different looks (the base site plus the game sites of config/game_sites.yml, each with its theme in app/assets/stylesheets/themes/). Any design change (CSS, view markup, new component, new theme) MUST be checked on ALL of them before it is proposed:
  - never hardcode a color, a font or an image in a view, a component, an inline style or a Stimulus controller: use the tokens declared in the `:root` of app/assets/stylesheets/application.css (`--surface`, `--text-strong`, `--highlight-bg`...). If no token fits, add one in application.css with its base value, and set it in EVERY theme file
  - themes are both dark (epic_ferc) and light (trench_crusade, confrontation, base site): check that text stays readable on its background in each of them
  - render the changed pages on the base site and on every game site (set `GAME_SITES="epic.localhost=epic_ferc,tc.localhost=trench_crusade,conf.localhost=confrontation"` and browse http://epic.localhost:3000 etc.), on desktop and mobile widths, and state in your answer which sites and pages were checked
  - when adding a game site, follow "Adding a game site" in context.md; test/integration/game_site_themes_test.rb must pass
  - only use images and fonts we have the rights to: the trench_crusade theme must not embed any artwork or font of the game or of trenchcrusade.fr
- always use localization, never use hardcoded strings. always populated both French and English locales. The only exception is the admin dashboard (/avo) that does not need to be localized and is coded only in english

# Style

- use the hash syntax for method params. For example, use Mailer.notify(subject: xxx, content: yyy) instead of Mailer.notify(xxx, yyy)

